{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfqDnMvuz5vYMseCNfZF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Model.** I treated the complex as high-spin octahedral d⁷ (Co²⁺-type) with a ⁴T₁g(F) ground state. The three bands are then:\n\n- ν₁ = ⁴T₁g → ⁴T₂g\n- ν₂ = ⁴T₁g → ⁴A₂g\n- ν₃ = ⁴T₁g → ⁴T₁g(P)\n\n**Energies.** Relative to the ⁴F term, ⁴T₂g lies at 2Dq and ⁴A₂g at 12Dq. The two ⁴T₁g states mix through the matrix [[−6Dq, 6B],[6B, 15B+6Dq]]. The lower eigenvalue is the ground state and the upper one is ⁴T₁g(P).\n\n**Fit.** I assigned the bands in the order given (8 620, 18 410, 20 250 cm⁻¹). There are three bands and only two parameters, so I fitted Dq and B by least squares. The three band energies are not exactly consistent with this model. An exact solution from ν₁ and ν₂ alone gives Dq ≈ 979 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 462 cm⁻¹, but that solution does not reproduce ν₃.\n\nThe least-squares fit gives Dq ≈ 1000 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 490 cm⁻¹. The residuals (calculated minus observed) are about −165 cm⁻¹ for ν₁, +67 cm⁻¹ for ν₂ and +8 cm⁻¹ for ν₃. That is a reasonable fit, and B is about 50% of the free-ion value for Co²⁺.\n\nThe spin-allowed bands depend only on Dq and B, so C cannot be determined from them.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1000, \"B\": 490, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Least-squares fit of the d7 quartet Tanabe–Sugano matrix gives Dq≈1000 and B≈490 cm^-1; C is not determined by spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1244,"cache_read_input_tokens":1659,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1244,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5dd0e246-7620-45e9-b450-5a2ad3f297ab","uuid":"8bacfdbe-7668-4c5